From 83e73d9b4c7c56c07b1ff182862f6424934bb516 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philipp Grulich <pgrulichhh@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 10:28:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Lower casts to bool as value != 0 in the AsmJit, TBC and MLIR
 backends

A cast to bool must follow C++ semantics (value != 0), but:
- AsmJit (x64 and AArch64) truncated the value to its low byte, so
  256 or 0.5 became false; x64 constant folding did the same.
- TBC had no lowering for casts to bool and threw "unsupported cast".
- MLIR threw for float -> bool (integer -> bool already used cmpi ne).

AsmJit now tests the source width against zero and sets the flag (for
floats, ucomis + setne/setp on x64, fcmp #0 + cset ne on AArch64, so NaN is
true); its constant folding yields value != 0. TBC emits NE against a zero
slot. MLIR uses cmpf une against 0.0.

Adds a cast-to-bool test for all backends except bc, which still has no
lowering for these casts. The previous to_bool section in createCastTest
stays commented out, as it also runs the interpreter-only min/max values
through bc.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
